  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 233 AM CDT Wed Jul 29 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nSurface high pressure is being shunted off to the southeast today. \nThis will allow for southwesterly winds to stream back into the area \npromoting increased temperatures and dewpoints. To account for this \nwarmer weather we have issued a Heat Advisory for most of the \nNorthland. Precipitation chances today remain low. There remains \npoor agreement among guidance but given the weakly forced \nenvironment plus the smoke aloft, storm generation may be hard to \ncome by. \n\nTomorrow:\n\nThe upper level ridge that has been over the region begins to break \ndown early tomorrow morning as a shortwave trough advances in from \nthe west. This initial wave may lead to a few showers and storms \nacross our the International Border in the early morning hours. \nThrough the day a deeper trough will be advancing across the \nNorthern Plains with it's attendant cold front marching east. These \nfeatures are set to move through the Northland late Thursday into \nFriday which is when we have our highest chances for precipitation \nand storms. Severe threat remains marginal as highlighted by the \nSPC. Plenty of instability (2000 J/kg) to work with but bulk shear \nmay be lacking for severe storms to be sustained. Steep low level \nlapse rates paired with high DCAPE seems to favor an environment for \ndamaging winds for any strong storms that do manage to develop. \nStill a lot of spread in the high res guidance, particularly with \nthe storm evolution in the morning prior to the onset of the strong \ntrough.\n\nFriday/Weekend:\n\nRainfall chances will then linger into Friday before drying out for \nthe weekend. The jet stream will then shift north of the border for \nthe weekend and into next week, keeping the main storm track to our \nnorth. There will be limited chances for precipitation, but nothing \nsignificant at this point. Temperatures cool into the 70s and low \n80s for Friday and Saturday before slowly warming heading into the \nnew week.",
